======================================================================================================================
PART B -- THE DECISIVE STRUCTURAL QUESTION.  Does 'the excess can only be spent once' generalise?
======================================================================================================================

L55's theorem is scoped to THIS action's kernel combined with a LambdaCDM-profile cold abundance.
Its argument, stripped of the action: the galaxy anomaly is ONE NUMBER PER POINT, and a component
present in the amount the CMB requires already spends it.  That argument mentions neither the mode
count, nor the number of metrics, nor the matter coupling.  Below it is restated as a theorem with
explicit hypotheses, and each hypothesis is then tested against what the three branches can do.

  THEOREM (excess spent once -- branch-independent form).  Let a theory predict, at each point of a
  galaxy, the acceleration felt by a test baryon as

        g_pred  =  g_bar  +  A_X  +  A_K

  with g_bar the Newtonian field of the OBSERVED baryons, A_X the acceleration transmitted to that
  baryon by a pressureless component present in the amount the CMB fixes, and A_K everything else the
  modification supplies.  Assume
      (H-a)  NO CANCELLATION:  A_X >= 0 and A_K >= 0 pointwise (both are inward);
      (H-b)  MOND NORMALISATION:  with A_X = 0 the theory reproduces the observed relation, i.e. in the
             deep regime A_K -> sqrt(g_bar a0) = the measured anomaly A = g_obs - g_bar;
      (H-c)  TRANSMISSION:  A_X = eta * g_halo with eta > 0, where g_halo is the Newtonian field of the
             cold component at the abundance the CMB fixes.
  Then  g_pred - g_obs = A_X >= 0 pointwise: the theory OVERSHOOTS the measured rotation curve by
  exactly the cold component's transmitted pull.  Admissibility therefore requires either eta small,
  or the kernel suppressed by s_eff, and in the deep regime a suppression s is EXACTLY degenerate with
  a smaller acceleration scale, a0' = s^2 a0.

  The conclusion depends on the number of propagating modes, the number of metrics and the matter
  coupling ONLY through (H-a) and (H-c).  Everything else is arithmetic on measured accelerations.

B.1  The inequality, checked pointwise on SPARC rather than asserted.

         eta |        eps=1 (total-sourced) |       eps=0 (baryon-sourced)
             |      rms can/alt  median can |      rms can/alt  median can
        0.00 |   0.145/  0.142      +0.030 |   0.145/  0.142      +0.030
        0.20 |   0.169/  0.184      -0.055 |   0.146/  0.153      -0.024
        0.35 |   0.206/  0.225      -0.109 |   0.162/  0.174      -0.057
        0.50 |   0.241/  0.262      -0.150 |   0.184/  0.197      -0.092
        0.58 |   0.259/  0.280      -0.170 |   0.197/  0.210      -0.110
        0.75 |   0.293/  0.315      -0.208 |   0.224/  0.238      -0.145
        1.00 |   0.338/  0.360      -0.259 |   0.264/  0.277      -0.191
      at eta = 1, deep-MOND points (2059 of 2786): median g_pred/g_obs = 1.692 = +0.228 dex overshoot
  [PASS] B1  the overshoot is pointwise and equals the transmitted halo term (no cancellation is possible with A_X >= 0)   (median g_pred/g_obs = 1.692 at eta = 1 with the WEAKEST (baryon-sourced) kernel)

B.2  The ceiling on eta.  This is the number every branch has to hit, and it is computed from the data
     and the RAR's own scatter, not from any action.  Criterion: the median RAR residual stays inside
     the relation's own 0.11 dex (the GENEROUS of L49's two criteria, carried so no deficit is manufactured).

      eps = 1 (kernel reads the total potential):   eta <= 0.355 / 0.276     (L49 reports 0.355 / 0.276)
      eps = 0 (kernel reads the baryons only):      eta <= 0.582 / 0.486     (L50 reports 0.582 / 0.486)
      the CMB requires eta = 1.000 +- 0.010 at recombination (Omega_c h^2 = 0.1200 +- 0.0012)
  [PASS] B2  control: the transmission ceiling reproduces L49's and L50's published cold-fraction windows   (eta and the cold fraction f enter the same way, so the ceiling IS their window)
  [PASS] B3  the requirement any branch must meet: transmit at most ~58% / ~49% of the CMB-required cold component's pull at SPARC radii, while transmitting ~100% at recombination   (eta <= 0.582 / 0.486 in galaxies vs 1.000 at z = 1100)

B.3  The ONLY escape is to break (H-c): make baryons blind to the cold component INSIDE galaxies while
     leaving the CMB's driving intact.  Two mechanism families exist for that, and both are tested.

     (i) A RANGE (a mass term / graviton mass / Yukawa mediator).  Required: suppression at ~10 kpc,
         NO suppression at the sound horizon ~145 Mpc comoving.  A Yukawa force ratio is
         (1 + m r) exp(-m r), STRICTLY DECREASING in r: it suppresses LONG range and not short.
      physical sound horizon at z = 1100:  r_s = 0.1317 Mpc  (comoving 145 Mpc)
      tune the mass so that eta(10 kpc) = 0.582:   m r_gal = 1.429, Compton wavelength 7.00 kpc;   then eta(r_s) = 1.337e-07  -- the CMB's driving is switched off too
      tune the mass so that eta(r_s)  >= 0.90:              m r_s = 0.532, Compton wavelength 0.248 Mpc;   then eta(10 kpc) = 0.999206  -- the galaxy is not suppressed at all
  [PASS] B4  a mass/range mechanism CANNOT break (H-c): the Yukawa ratio is monotone decreasing, so it suppresses long range and not short -- the opposite ordering to the one required   (eta(r_s) = 1.34e-07 when eta(10 kpc) = 0.582; eta(10 kpc) = 0.999206 when eta(r_s) = 0.90)

     (ii) An ENVIRONMENT (a screening function of a local scalar: density, acceleration, potential).
          This is L6's class read in the mirror -- L6 asked whether a monotone S(X) could ENHANCE
          clusters relative to galaxies; the question here is whether one could SUPPRESS the cold
          component's pull in galaxies relative to recombination.  L6's own cosmological-ordering horn
          is the test, and it is recomputed here for the recombination epoch, which L6 never used.

      mean matter density at recombination        rho = 3.587e-18 kg/m^3
      L* galaxy at 10 kpc, halo + MIDPLANE baryons rho = 7.199e-21 kg/m^3   -- recombination is 498x DENSER  (the conservative comparison)
      the same galaxy at 30 kpc, halo only         rho = 6.777e-23 kg/m^3   -- recombination is 52939x denser
      cluster at 1000 kpc                          rho = 2.836e-24 kg/m^3
  [PASS] B5  DENSITY-keyed screening has the WRONG ORDERING: the recombination-era universe is denser than a galaxy where the anomaly is measured -- even counting the disc's own midplane baryons -- so any monotone suppression in dense regions suppresses the CMB's driving harder than the galaxy's   (rho_rec/rho_gal = 498 (conservative), 52939 at 30 kpc)
      
      acoustic driving at Phi = 1e-05:  g ~ c^2 Phi / r_s = 2.212e-10 m/s^2 = 2.36 / 1.96 a0
      acoustic driving at Phi = 3e-05:  g ~ c^2 Phi / r_s = 6.635e-10 m/s^2 = 7.09 / 5.88 a0
      acoustic driving at Phi = 1e-04:  g ~ c^2 Phi / r_s = 2.212e-09 m/s^2 = 23.62 / 19.61 a0
      median SPARC acceleration                    g = 6.834e-11 m/s^2 = 0.73 / 0.61 a0
      clusters at R500 sit at 0.33-0.58 a0 (this repository's own recorded value), i.e. AT OR BELOW
      galactic accelerations -- and clusters require MORE transmission (eta 0.569-0.893), not less.
  [PASS] B6  ACCELERATION-keyed screening fails twice: the CMB's own driving acceleration is within ~1 dex of galactic accelerations (no clean separation), and clusters sit at or BELOW galactic accelerations while requiring MORE transmission (wrong ordering)   (|log10(g_rec/g_gal)| = 0.99 dex; clusters 0.45 a0 vs galaxies 0.73 a0)
      potential depth:  galaxy |Phi|/c^2 = 2.50e-07,  cluster = 1.60e-05, recombination perturbation = 3.0e-05
      clusters require eta = 0.569 +- 0.130 (b = 0) to 0.893 +- 0.158 (b = 0.20) [L50 D4/D10]; a monotone
      suppression in DEEP potentials suppresses clusters MORE than galaxies, and clusters need MORE.
  [PASS] B7  POTENTIAL-keyed screening has the wrong ordering between galaxies and clusters: clusters are 64x deeper and require MORE transmission, not less   (Phi_clu/Phi_gal = 64)

B.4  The other half of the trade: suppress the KERNEL instead of the halo.  In the deep regime a
     uniform suppression s is exactly degenerate with a smaller acceleration scale.
     s * a0 * Delta(g/a0)  ==  a0' * Delta(g/a0')  with  a0' = s^2 a0, checked in the strict limit.
      deep-limit degeneracy check at g/a0 = 1e-08, s = 0.438:  relative error 6.42e-05
      at eta = 1 the kernel must be suppressed to s <= 0.495 / 0.439  (L50 D8 / L55 D4 report 0.495 / 0.439)
      which puts the surviving term's own MOND transition at a0' <= 0.245 a0 = 2.290e-11 / 2.169e-11 m/s^2
  [PASS] B8  control: the kernel-suppression ceiling reproduces L50 D8 / L55 D4 and the a0' = s^2 a0 degeneracy   (s <= 0.495/0.439, a0' <= 0.245 a0)

B.5  THE VERDICT ON GENERALISATION.

     Every step used: (1) measured accelerations at SPARC radii; (2) Omega_c h^2 from the CMB;
     (3) the assumption that both contributions pull inward; (4) the theory's own MOND normalisation.
     NONE of them mentions the number of propagating modes, the number of metrics, or how matter
     couples.  Those enter only through the transmission factor eta, and the two mechanism families
     that could make eta scale- or environment-dependent both fail on ORDERING, not on magnitude:
     a range suppresses long and not short; density puts recombination on the WRONG side; acceleration
     does not separate the two epochs at all; potential puts clusters on the wrong side.
  [PASS] B9  [THE DECISIVE QUESTION]  the 'excess spent once' argument GENERALISES beyond one action: it is branch-independent, depending on modes / metrics / coupling only through the transmission factor   (proved as a theorem with hypotheses (H-a),(H-b),(H-c); the escape route is closed on ORDERING)

     >> THEOREM (branch-independent).  No relativistic theory that (a) reproduces the observed deep-MOND
     >> relation in galaxies with its cold component switched off, (b) contains a pressureless component
     >> in the amount the CMB requires, and (c) transmits that component's Newtonian pull to baryons
     >> with an efficiency that is not smaller inside galaxies than at recombination, can fit the
     >> measured rotation curves.  It overshoots by the transmitted pull, pointwise.
     >> The hypotheses that a branch could attack are (c) alone, and (c) requires a suppression whose
     >> ordering is short-range-off / long-range-on, or dense-off / dilute-on with recombination denser
     >> than a galaxy -- neither of which any mass term or monotone environmental screening supplies.
     >> SCOPE, stated: the cold component's GALAXY PROFILE is still an input (abundance-matched LambdaCDM
     >> here). A theory that puts the cold component somewhere other than in galaxy halos escapes -- and
     >> that is a statement about the dark sector's microphysics, closed separately by this repository's
     >> 2026-09-06/07 dark-sector no-go, NOT by this theorem.

======================================================================================================================
PART C -- BRANCH 1: three or more propagating modes, Lorentz invariant.  (RAQUAL, phase-coupling gravity)
======================================================================================================================

The theorem permits it: give up requirement (iii) and keep Lorentz invariance.  RAQUAL
(Bekenstein-Milgrom 1984) and phase-coupling gravity (Bekenstein 1988) are the two constructions in the
17-theory table, with N_grav = 3 and >= 3.  The brief's question: what does the extra mode COST, and are
their known failures GENERIC to the branch or specific to those two constructions?

C.0  What the extra mode has to buy.  L39's operator lemma (cited, not re-derived) says: with no
     preferred timelike vector there is exactly ONE transverse symmetric operator, so lensing and
     dynamics cannot be modified separately; adjoining one unit timelike vector makes the count two and
     unlocks them.  DC-013 (cited) says the same from diffeomorphism invariance: (Phi,Psi) are locked to
     the ray (1,-2) for a single-metric frame-free modification at ANY number of modes.  So a
     Lorentz-invariant extra mode cannot unlock lensing by modifying the metric sector.  The only
     remaining lever is the MATTER COUPLING, and that gives an exhaustive four-way fork:

       (1a) CONFORMAL  g~ = A(phi) g          -> tested here, C.1-C.3
       (1b) DISFORMAL  g~ = A g + B dphi dphi -> this is BRANCH 3; handed to PART E
       (1c) the field acquires a TIMELIKE GRADIENT vev (mimetic / Deffayet-Esposito-Farese-Woodard)
            -> that IS a preferred frame; L39 established it and it satisfies the theorem's conclusion
            rather than escaping it
       (1d) a VECTOR with a timelike vev (TeVeS, GEA, AeST) -> preferred frame, in L31's table already

     (1c) and (1d) are not escapes, they are instances of the conclusion.  (1b) leaves the branch.
     So branch 1's own content is (1a), and the question is whether (1a)'s failure is generic.

C.1  The generic obstruction, derived rather than quoted: a conformally coupled Lorentz-invariant
     scalar's own gravitating stress is smaller than the phantom it must mimic by the galaxy's OWN
     v^2/c^2.  This is not RAQUAL-specific -- it follows from the deep-MOND normalisation alone.

     scalar energy density      rho_phi     = X**(3/2)/(12*pi*G*a0*c**2)
     phantom density required   rho_phantom = sqrt(X)/(4*pi*G*r)
     ratio                                  = X*r/(3*a0*c**2)
     with |grad phi| = v_c^2/r              = vc**4/(3*a0*c**2*r)
  [PASS] C1  (symbolic) the ratio reduces to v_c^4 / (3 a0 r c^2) = (v_c^2/c^2) x (g_MOND / 3 a0): the scalar's gravitating stress is suppressed by the galaxy's own v^2/c^2   (derived from the deep-MOND normalisation, no kernel shape assumed)

      [canonical] rho_phi / rho_phantom on 2059 deep-MOND SPARC points: median 1.35e-08, max 1.95e-07
      [alt      ] rho_phi / rho_phantom on 2133 deep-MOND SPARC points: median 1.52e-08, max 1.95e-07
  [FAIL] C2  BRANCH 1, GATE 1 (lensing vs dynamics): does a conformally coupled Lorentz-invariant scalar supply an O(1) fraction of the lensing mass it must?   (NO -- median rho_phi/rho_phantom = 1.35e-08.  It lenses like general relativity on the baryons alone; the gate needs O(1))

      [canonical] predicted M_dyn / M_lens on deep-MOND points: median 3.30, 90th percentile 5.64    (observed: 1)
      [alt      ] predicted M_dyn / M_lens on deep-MOND points: median 3.46, 90th percentile 6.09    (observed: 1)
  [FAIL] C3  BRANCH 1, GATE 1, the size of the miss: is the predicted dynamics-to-lensing mass ratio consistent with the measured ~1?   (median M_dyn/M_lens = 3.30)

C.2  Is the failure GENERIC to the branch or specific to RAQUAL and PCG?  Three tests.
      the deposited saturating kernel       : median rho_phi/rho_phantom = 8.41e-09
      Milgrom simple  mu = y/(1+y)          : median rho_phi/rho_phantom = 8.59e-09
      Milgrom standard mu = y/sqrt(1+y^2)   : median rho_phi/rho_phantom = 4.90e-09
  [PASS] C4  the obstruction is KERNEL-INDEPENDENT: three different interpolating functions give the same suppression to within a factor of a few, because the scale is set by v^2/c^2 and not by f   (spread across kernels 1.75x, all below 1e-5)

      Second test -- the historical record agrees, and it agrees for this reason.  Bekenstein's own
      sequence RAQUAL -> PCG -> TeVeS was driven by exactly this: TeVeS's unit timelike vector was
      introduced FOR the light bending.  L39 quotes DEFW 2011 verbatim: 'That problem was finally
      surmounted in 2004 by ... TeVeS ... where the presence of a unit timelike vector field helps in
      obtaining the right amount of light deflection.'  The failure is the branch's, not the construction's.

      Third test -- the ROUTE OUT of (1a) is (1b), and (1b) is not in this branch.  There is no
      Lorentz-invariant conformal construction that evades C1, because C1 uses only the deep-MOND
      normalisation |grad phi| = sqrt(g_bar a0) and the fact that a conformal factor does not bend light (A8).
  [FAIL] C5  BRANCH 1 VERDICT: is it alive on gate 1 (lensing vs dynamics)?   (DEAD at the FIRST gate; the failure is generic, not RAQUAL-specific)

     WHAT THE EXTRA MODE COSTS, stated: it costs the whole lensing sector and buys nothing, because a
     Lorentz-invariant scalar cannot supply the second transverse operator L39's lemma requires (its
     gradient is spacelike in a static configuration; a timelike gradient vev is spontaneous Lorentz
     violation and is case (1c)).  The extra mode also carries the branch's own known costs -- RAQUAL's
     superluminal scalar propagation in the transition region, which is what drove Bekenstein to PCG --
     but those are downstream of a gate already closed and are cited, not computed here.

     NOT CLOSED BY THIS LANE: a Lorentz-invariant branch-1 theory whose matter coupling is disformal
     is handed to PART E; and DC-013's ray lock, which covers the non-conformal metric-sector route at
     any number of modes, is cited from the repository's record rather than re-derived.

======================================================================================================================
PART D -- BRANCH 2: two metrics.  (dRGT / Hassan-Rosen, BIMOND, and this repository's open subspace)
======================================================================================================================

READ THE RECORD FIRST, as the brief requires.  project_relativistic_mond_closure_2026 carries:
  * the SINGLE-METRIC pincer (DC-013 + DC-019), which explicitly does NOT cover bimetric;
  * DC-018: standard ghost-free dRGT / Hassan-Rosen bigravity's helicity-0 Galileon sector CANNOT
    give MOND's 1/r -- the spherical flux scaling theorem;
  * BIMOND connection interactions: Boulware-Deser ghost risk (the lapse RATIO, rank-1 Hessian);
  * THE ONE OPEN DOOR: the background-independent lapse-velocity-free (ghost-free) subspace of the
    COMPLETE 5-invariant derivative-bimetric basis is 2-D and contains MOND-alive directions off the
    f(Q) line; T4 - T1 gives static-NR MOND acceleration a = -4 AND lensing source b = -8, both
    nonzero.  HEALTH IS RECORDED AS UNDECIDED.  This lane does NOT re-close it and does not claim it.

D.1  DC-018 reproduced as a control (it is cheap and it calibrates the branch's first gate).

     flux relation r^(3-n) (pi')^n = GM  =>  pi' = (GM*r**(n - 3))**(1/n)
     radial exponent of pi'              =  (n - 3)/n
       n = 1:  pi' ~ r^(-2)
       n = 2:  pi' ~ r^(-1/2)
       n = 3:  pi' ~ r^(0)
       n = 4:  pi' ~ r^(1/4)
     MOND requires pi' ~ r^(-1)  =>  n = 3/2  -- NOT an integer
  [PASS] D1  control: DC-018 reproduced.  A spherical Galileon of integer order gives pi' ~ r^(1 - 3/n); MOND's r^(-1) needs the non-integer n = 3/2, so standard bigravity's helicity-0 sector has no MOND   (n = 3/2; integer n in {1,2,3,4} gives r^-2, r^-1/2, r^0, r^1/4)

D.2  The mode price.  Branch 2 is not disjoint from branch 1: a healthy second metric costs 5 extra
     propagating modes, so every two-metric theory is ALSO a >= 3-mode theory.
      ghost-free Hassan-Rosen:  N = 7 = 2 (massless) + 5 (massive graviton)
      with the Boulware-Deser mode unremoved:  N = 8, one of them a ghost
      the repository's open subspace is exactly the question of which of these two the a != 0 sub-family is
  [PASS] D2  branch 2 lies inside 'three or more modes': the permitted branches overlap and are not three independent directions   (7 healthy or 8 with the ghost; either way >= 3)

D.3  Does the branch-independent theorem of PART B follow the second metric?  This is the question that
     matters, because if it does the branch is dead before its gates are priced.

     A second metric offers exactly one new way to attack hypothesis (H-c): put the cold component on
     the SECOND metric, so that its pull reaches baryons only through the g-f interaction.  In every
     ghost-free bimetric construction that interaction is a graviton MASS TERM, so the transmitted force
     carries the Yukawa factor of B.3(i) -- and B4 already showed that factor has the wrong ordering.
